"this poor fellow isn't sharp."

259
00:09:30,733 --> 00:09:32,233
He's weary and confused.

260
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:33,867
He doesn't care about us,

261
00:09:33,867 --> 00:09:36,400
and he probably doesn't know
what he's talking about.

262
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:38,767
And you,
you are puzzled.

263
00:09:38,767 --> 00:09:40,833
Why has your speech failed?

264
00:09:40,833 --> 00:09:43,200
Uh, ever seen
a one-eared elephant?

265
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:45,333
Mister, you don't look good,
that's why.

266
00:09:45,333 --> 00:09:47,867
What your audience sees
is so bad,

267
00:09:47,867 --> 00:09:49,367
they can't hear
what you're saying.

268
00:09:49,367 --> 00:09:52,233
He's got Patty Duke's dad
in his contact lens.

269
00:09:52,233 --> 00:09:53,733
Now, good platform posture

270
00:09:53,733 --> 00:09:55,667
can be accomplished
by anyone

271
00:09:55,667 --> 00:09:57,900
with a little effort
and concentration.

272
00:09:57,900 --> 00:10:00,367
Good posture is,
first, a physical matter.

273
00:10:00,367 --> 00:10:04,467
The body should be held erect
and tall, with the head up.

274
00:10:04,467 --> 00:10:07,900
Secondly, good posture
is a matter of the mind.

275
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:09,300
Your mind.

276
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:11,667
In your thinking,
you must convince yourself

277
00:10:11,667 --> 00:10:16,833
you will think tall, talk tall,
stand tall, and walk tall.

278
00:10:16,833 --> 00:10:20,467
Shave tall,
jump tall, and crouch tall.

279
00:10:20,467 --> 00:10:22,533
Remember that you
must think tall.

280
00:10:22,533 --> 00:10:23,533
Mm-hmm.

281
00:10:23,533 --> 00:10:25,233
Talk tall.

282
00:10:25,233 --> 00:10:26,733
Stand tall.

283
00:10:26,733 --> 00:10:28,067
And walk tall.

284
00:10:28,067 --> 00:10:30,233
This will help you with your
posture and your speech.

285
00:10:30,233 --> 00:10:33,367
What if you're
Robert reich?

286
00:10:33,367 --> 00:10:36,067
Now, good posture serves
more of a purpose

287
00:10:36,067 --> 00:10:37,767
than just
making you look better.

288
00:10:37,767 --> 00:10:39,567
It helps you to be
comfortable

289
00:10:39,567 --> 00:10:42,100
because it keeps you
well-balanced on the platform.

290
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:43,967
America first!

291
00:10:43,967 --> 00:10:47,400
Poor balance, along
with poor posture, causes

292
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:50,433
some of the characteristic types
of speakers you have seen.

293
00:10:50,433 --> 00:10:52,400
This type knows his subject

294
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:56,067
and has good control
of his voice, but look at him.

295
00:10:56,067 --> 00:10:58,833
He looks almost too tired
to stand up.

296
00:10:58,833 --> 00:11:01,333
Women be different than men!

297
00:11:01,333 --> 00:11:02,567
Word.

298
00:11:02,567 --> 00:11:05,533
He balances
first on one leg,

299
00:11:05,533 --> 00:11:06,633
then on the other.

300
00:11:06,633 --> 00:11:07,633
Ahh.

301
00:11:07,633 --> 00:11:09,533
- Ooh!
- [Wolf whistle]

302
00:11:09,533 --> 00:11:11,900
That's really very good.

303
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:14,100
He's the tired
farm horse type.

304
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:15,900
She's an energetic city girl.

305
00:11:15,900 --> 00:11:18,300
Can they get along
in the suburbs?

306
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,233
Then there's miss prim,

307
00:11:20,233 --> 00:11:23,867
the speaker who stands straight,
tall, and rigid.

308
00:11:23,867 --> 00:11:25,833
She seems to be afraid
she'll break,

309
00:11:25,833 --> 00:11:28,467
and she looks frightened
and excessively formal.

310
00:11:28,467 --> 00:11:30,967
And yet startlingly erotic.
[Giggles]

311
00:11:30,967 --> 00:11:33,567
♪ Wouldn't it be loverly ♪

312
00:11:33,567 --> 00:11:36,133
She keeps her feet
clamped tightly together.

313
00:11:36,133 --> 00:11:37,367
'Nuff said.

314
00:11:37,367 --> 00:11:40,233
We know her
as the telephone pole type.

315
00:11:40,233 --> 00:11:42,233
♪ Hi-yo-ta-ho ha! ♪

316
00:11:42,233 --> 00:11:44,833
Next, we have the he-man
athletic type.

317
00:11:44,833 --> 00:11:46,367
- Grandpa?
- You've often seen him.

318
00:11:46,367 --> 00:11:49,300
He stands with his shoulders
back and his stomach out

319
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:52,067
and his feet wide apart.

320
00:11:52,067 --> 00:11:55,433
-He looks undignified,
and soon his awkward posture

321
00:11:55,433 --> 00:11:57,100
- tires him...
- Il duce.

322
00:11:57,100 --> 00:11:59,167
...and he rocks back and forth
for relief.

323
00:11:59,167 --> 00:12:01,767
The lions sense his weakness
and close in,

324
00:12:01,767 --> 00:12:03,033
chewing at his hamstrings.

325
00:12:03,033 --> 00:12:06,667
[Imitating creaking noise]

326
00:12:06,667 --> 00:12:08,700
He is
the rocking horse type.

327
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:10,367
She's a straight-back chair
type.

328
00:12:10,367 --> 00:12:11,500
Can they get along?

329
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:14,000
Lastly, we come to the type
who leans.

330
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,467
He leans on the table,

331
00:12:15,467 --> 00:12:17,600
back of a chair,
or anything handy.

332
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:20,167
In trying to be
nonchalant and at ease,

333
00:12:20,167 --> 00:12:22,633
he makes himself thoroughly
uncomfortable.

334
00:12:22,633 --> 00:12:24,167
The belching
doesn't help, either.

335
00:12:24,167 --> 00:12:27,533
Plus, he's leaning on the people
in the front row.

336
00:12:27,533 --> 00:12:29,333
He is the turtle type.

337
00:12:29,333 --> 00:12:32,267
All four common types
look awkward,

338
00:12:32,267 --> 00:12:35,033
partially because
they're off-balance.

339
00:12:35,033 --> 00:12:37,700
Good balance results
from having the bodily weight

340
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:39,433
well-distributed
on both feet,

341
00:12:39,433 --> 00:12:41,933
and in having the feet
just the right distance apart

342
00:12:41,933 --> 00:12:43,700
- for comfort.
- [Wolf whistle]

343
00:12:43,700 --> 00:12:45,767
You can determine this distance
by making the knee test.

344
00:12:45,767 --> 00:12:48,600
I will not
make the knee test.

345
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:51,333
Place the palms of your hands
on both knees.

346
00:12:51,333 --> 00:12:52,767
Wait, I'm kidding!

347
00:12:52,767 --> 00:12:55,567
Make an easy circular motion
with the hands and knees.

348
00:12:55,567 --> 00:12:57,333
Until you're
incredibly turned on.

349
00:12:57,333 --> 00:12:59,700
If your feet are the correct
distance apart,

350
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:01,733
the weight will be
evenly distributed

351
00:13:01,733 --> 00:13:03,367
as you change position.

352
00:13:03,367 --> 00:13:05,567
You'll be well balanced
and comfortable.

353
00:13:05,567 --> 00:13:07,200
You'll look
poised and dignified.

354
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:08,500
Uh, no, you won't.

355
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:10,400
With your feet
too far apart,

356
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:12,167
you'll have difficulty
in standing up.

357
00:13:12,167 --> 00:13:14,033
Because you're off-balance,

358
00:13:14,033 --> 00:13:16,333
you may fall either forward
or backward.

359
00:13:16,333 --> 00:13:19,267
Don't do this
during the speech.

360
00:13:19,267 --> 00:13:21,433
With your feet
too close together,

361
00:13:21,433 --> 00:13:24,233
you're still off balance
and you're uncomfortable.

362
00:13:24,233 --> 00:13:26,033
That's fine...
Security!

363
00:13:26,033 --> 00:13:27,733
That's really nice.
Security!

364
00:13:27,733 --> 00:13:30,133
Uh, is your speech over,
Mr. Johnson?

365
00:13:30,133 --> 00:13:33,700
Uh, good.

366
00:13:33,700 --> 00:13:36,067
Try it until you find
the easiest

367
00:13:36,067 --> 00:13:37,900
and most
comfortable position,

368
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:39,833
then note the location
of your feet.

369
00:13:39,833 --> 00:13:42,333
Yeah, that's it, baby,
shake that moneymaker!

370
00:13:42,333 --> 00:13:44,100
Always try to keep your feet
in this position

371
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:45,367
when you are speaking,

372
00:13:45,367 --> 00:13:47,300
and you won't have
to worry about balance.

373
00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:48,733
And with good balance,

374
00:13:48,733 --> 00:13:51,400
you've conquered another phase
of good appearance.

375
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:55,167
[Humming theme
from "my three sons"]

376
00:13:55,167 --> 00:13:57,433
Now remember that appearance
is important.

377
00:13:57,433 --> 00:13:59,467
You must appear,
and you must have matter.

378
00:13:59,467 --> 00:14:01,833
And that good appearance
is easily accomplished.

379
00:14:01,833 --> 00:14:03,000
Except by Denny Dillon.

380
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,633
Just remember
to think tall...

381
00:14:05,633 --> 00:14:07,067
Talk tall...

382
00:14:07,067 --> 00:14:08,733
Stand tall...

383
00:14:08,733 --> 00:14:11,433
And walk tall,
and to achieve good balance.

384
00:14:11,433 --> 00:14:13,400
For good posture
and appearance

385
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:15,700
will greatly increase
your ability as a speaker

386
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:16,967
and a leader.

387
00:14:16,967 --> 00:14:20,067
And remember to always leave
your area at home.

388
00:14:20,067 --> 00:14:21,400
My posture is good!

389
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:24,667
[Screeches like an eagle]

390
00:14:24,667 --> 00:14:25,700
Eagle.

391
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:37,267
No, cut, no.
Do it again.

392
00:14:37,267 --> 00:14:39,267
Well, this is a sunny opening.

393
00:14:39,267 --> 00:14:40,767
Mm-hmm.

394
00:14:40,767 --> 00:14:43,267
Says, "lutheran
pacific railroad."

395
00:14:43,267 --> 00:14:46,033
- Hmm.
- Hmm.

396
00:14:46,033 --> 00:14:49,767
To get a good job,
get a good education.

397
00:14:49,767 --> 00:14:51,500
Um, hello?

398
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:53,100
Do you have a brunch?

399
00:14:54,500 --> 00:14:55,900
John carradine.

400
00:14:55,900 --> 00:14:57,800
Was he always 100 years old?

401
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:00,333
Mm-hmm.

402
00:15:00,333 --> 00:15:03,433
John carradine for viceroy.

403
00:15:03,433 --> 00:15:06,767
Sal mineo for viceroy.

404
00:15:09,500 --> 00:15:13,000
I'll keep walking, and
eventually they'll stop filming.

405
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,267
John really fills out a pair
of overalls, doesn't he?

406
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:23,267
Hmm, station is really bustling
this time of day.

407
00:15:25,700 --> 00:15:28,233
Huh?

408
00:15:28,233 --> 00:15:29,967
Mr. Wilson?

409
00:15:29,967 --> 00:15:31,033
That's right.

410
00:15:31,033 --> 00:15:33,100
What can I
do for you, young man?

411
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:36,067
My name's Jim Benton,
from the gazette.

412
00:15:36,067 --> 00:15:38,400
I'm doing a follow-up story

413
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:42,333
on the desperados
that were through here in '61.

414
00:15:42,333 --> 00:15:43,767
Ah, yes, yes.

415
00:15:43,767 --> 00:15:45,133
Which century?

416
00:15:45,133 --> 00:15:46,833
I remember those men.

417
00:15:46,833 --> 00:15:49,433
Griffin, cook, and landis.

418
00:15:49,433 --> 00:15:50,933
I believe you were
engineer

419
00:15:50,933 --> 00:15:52,500
on the train
they grabbed that night.

420
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:54,233
Yeah, that's right.

421
00:15:54,233 --> 00:15:55,267
Choo-choo Charlie
was my name.

422
00:15:55,267 --> 00:15:57,833
I was making
a run Albuquerque.

423
00:15:57,833 --> 00:15:59,533
That was in '61.

424
00:15:59,533 --> 00:16:01,333
Then I joined
the crash test dummies.

425
00:16:01,333 --> 00:16:04,367
Did you happen to see the men
in the yard that night, sir?

426
00:16:04,367 --> 00:16:07,500
No, at least not
close enough to recognize them.

427
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:10,033
As I pulled out
of the yard that night,

428
00:16:10,033 --> 00:16:11,967
I remember looking back
for the conductor's signal.

429
00:16:11,967 --> 00:16:14,400
I saw some men running.

430
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:16,400
It was dark.

431
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:18,900
Yes, sir?

432
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:20,633
That's not enough?

433
00:16:20,633 --> 00:16:21,633
[Chuckles]

434
00:16:21,633 --> 00:16:24,900
That seems like
a long time ago.

435
00:16:24,900 --> 00:16:26,833
Kid looks like a reporter
from the "catholic digest."

436
00:16:26,833 --> 00:16:27,933
Griffin.

437
00:16:27,933 --> 00:16:32,100
John makes Keith Richards
look dewy.

438
00:16:32,100 --> 00:16:34,900
He ran all the way to hell.

439
00:16:34,900 --> 00:16:38,167
But there's always
a stop in wausau.

440
00:16:38,167 --> 00:16:40,733
How's that fit you,
fancy pants?

441
00:16:40,733 --> 00:16:43,000
♪♪

442
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
The red zone is for Cuba
and UN-Cuba only.

443
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,433
You know, I've got
an oily Cuba zone.

444
00:16:53,733 --> 00:16:57,167
Coleman Francis is curly Howard
in "the fugitive."

445
00:16:57,167 --> 00:16:58,833
Hey, moe!

446
00:16:58,833 --> 00:17:00,967
Oh, he's good in anything.

447
00:17:00,967 --> 00:17:02,500
Mm-hmm.

448
00:17:04,367 --> 00:17:05,733
What's that music?
What the--

449
00:17:05,733 --> 00:17:08,833
oh, I'm being filmed, oh.

450
00:17:08,833 --> 00:17:12,300
This was just after he was
drained of life by the succubus.

451
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:17,367
♪ Night train to mundo fine ♪

452
00:17:17,367 --> 00:17:19,533
Well, they don't call
John carradine "the voice"

453
00:17:19,533 --> 00:17:20,700
for nothing.

454
00:17:20,700 --> 00:17:22,900
♪ Night train to the end ♪

455
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:24,967
Oh, to be blessed with
an instrument like that.

456
00:17:24,967 --> 00:17:28,067
♪ Running hard
and running fast ♪

457
00:17:28,067 --> 00:17:31,400
♪ to meet my future
and away from my past ♪

458
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:34,333
♪ taking the gamble
that cannot last ♪

459
00:17:34,333 --> 00:17:38,300
♪ night train to the end ♪

460
00:17:38,300 --> 00:17:40,733
Ha, he's trying
to sneak into China.

461
00:17:40,733 --> 00:17:44,500
♪ Hell's ride to mundo fine ♪

462
00:17:44,500 --> 00:17:45,700
[Whistling]

463
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:47,600
- Low keys.
- Hey.

464
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:52,400
♪ Hell's ride to the end ♪

465
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:54,133
Ho, now, I'm rocking.

466
00:17:54,133 --> 00:17:56,833
♪ Sold my soul
to the devil's bend ♪

467
00:17:56,833 --> 00:18:00,033
♪ drawing me hard with
a merciless hand ♪

468
00:18:00,033 --> 00:18:03,867
♪ and all I bought
is a handful of sand ♪

469
00:18:03,867 --> 00:18:08,967
♪ night train to the end ♪

470
00:18:08,967 --> 00:18:10,133
Whoops, there
we go again.

471
00:18:10,133 --> 00:18:15,733
♪ Night train to the end ♪

472
00:18:15,733 --> 00:18:17,800
Jeez, this escape route
is used a lot.

473
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:21,800
There's all the malt cups
from his previous takes.

474
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:24,500
Shake it!
Get down, now, boogie man!

475
00:18:24,500 --> 00:18:26,733
Whoa!
Play that funky music.

476
00:18:26,733 --> 00:18:27,800
♪ Drink night train ♪

477
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:29,733
♪ go to the basketball game ♪

478
00:18:29,733 --> 00:18:32,067
♪ throw up
under the bleachers ♪

479
00:18:32,067 --> 00:18:33,533
Moe is gonna kill me!

480
00:18:33,533 --> 00:18:35,133
Ow, ow, ow...

481
00:18:37,533 --> 00:18:40,900
We join our story,
already in progress.

482
00:18:43,633 --> 00:18:44,767
[Grunting like curly]

483
00:18:44,767 --> 00:18:47,033
Lord be with you.

484
00:18:47,033 --> 00:18:48,067
[Crickets chirping]

485
00:18:48,067 --> 00:18:50,633
Changing a tire
without a license, huh?

486
00:18:50,633 --> 00:18:51,800
[Metal clanks]

487
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:53,867
Move back.

488
00:18:53,867 --> 00:18:55,967
Come here, you.

489
00:18:55,967 --> 00:18:57,333
You look good,
been working out?

490
00:18:57,333 --> 00:18:58,333
What's your name?

491
00:18:58,333 --> 00:18:59,367
Cook.

492
00:18:59,367 --> 00:19:00,967
All right,
stand back there, cook.

493
00:19:00,967 --> 00:19:04,167
There'll be no tire changing
in my county, mister.

494
00:19:04,167 --> 00:19:06,000
[Giggling]
That tickles!

495
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:07,000
What's your name?

496
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:08,067
Landis.

497
00:19:08,067 --> 00:19:09,067
Where you going?

498
00:19:09,067 --> 00:19:10,333
Looking for work.

499
00:19:10,333 --> 00:19:12,100
We follow the harvest.

500
00:19:12,100 --> 00:19:14,833
Crops froze up north,
we're heading south.

501
00:19:14,833 --> 00:19:17,467
No work to be found nowhere,
officer.

502
00:19:17,467 --> 00:19:19,000
You seen anyone
around here?

503
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:20,633
Uh, the field, maybe?

504
00:19:20,633 --> 00:19:23,367
No, we ain't,
officer.

505
00:19:23,367 --> 00:19:26,033
I'll have to run you
through the computer.

506
00:19:26,033 --> 00:19:28,033
Well, I would if I had one.

507
00:19:28,033 --> 00:19:30,033
Crap, I'm locked
out of my car.

508
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:34,167
Crotch cops.

509
00:19:34,167 --> 00:19:36,467
♪ This is my country ♪

510
00:19:36,467 --> 00:19:39,767
Attention, all cars report
to three forks, immediately.

511
00:19:39,767 --> 00:19:43,033
What did we do?

512
00:19:43,033 --> 00:19:44,767
Boy, he's nothing
if not thorough.

513
00:19:44,767 --> 00:19:47,000
All right, get that truck out
of here or I'll run you both in!

514
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,267
Oh, so it's martial law
in rat's ass Missouri.

515
00:19:49,267 --> 00:19:50,700
See what happened?
Get out of my way.

516
00:19:50,700 --> 00:19:52,367
Well, you're the one
who wanted to stop here.

517
00:19:52,367 --> 00:19:53,867
I don't know why you had to stop
here in the first place.

518
00:19:53,867 --> 00:19:55,233
Ow!
I fell on my hubcap.

519
00:19:55,233 --> 00:19:57,267
Stupid, get in the stupid
truck...

520
00:19:57,267 --> 00:19:59,333
We just gotta get this thing
on and then we'll--

521
00:19:59,333 --> 00:20:00,533
shut up, shh, no!

522
00:20:00,533 --> 00:20:03,600
Damn tire changers!

523
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:05,000
[Dogs barking]

524
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,300
Sometimes walking the dog
just isn't that relaxing.

525
00:20:09,200 --> 00:20:13,033
Oh, Tony Curtis and Sidney
poitier are in there already.

526
00:20:16,767 --> 00:20:18,067
You know,
this movie can't decide

527
00:20:18,067 --> 00:20:21,033
if it's lighthearted comedy
or gripping drama.

528
00:20:21,033 --> 00:20:22,700
♪♪

529
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:24,000
Snausages...
Ow, my area!

530
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,333
[All howling]

531
00:20:25,333 --> 00:20:28,600
Thanks, now I'm barren!
Hoo hoo!

532
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:31,533
Phew! It smells like
Coleman Francis in here.

533
00:20:31,533 --> 00:20:34,133
[Yawns] I'm tired,
been a long day.

534
00:20:34,133 --> 00:20:36,267
[Feigns snoring]

535
00:20:36,267 --> 00:20:39,067
♪ Running down the road
trying to loosen my load ♪

536
00:20:39,067 --> 00:20:42,633
♪ I got Coleman Francis
on my mind ♪

537
00:20:42,633 --> 00:20:45,033
Well, this film
wastes no precious screen time

538
00:20:45,033 --> 00:20:46,767
with a plot.

539
00:20:46,767 --> 00:20:49,700
♪♪

540
00:20:54,433 --> 00:20:55,700
- Oh!
- Yeah.

541
00:20:55,700 --> 00:20:58,433
Well, this film is moving along
quite briskly.

542
00:20:58,433 --> 00:20:59,467
♪♪

543
00:20:59,467 --> 00:21:01,433
♪♪ [humming]

544
00:21:01,433 --> 00:21:03,167
How's your pasta?

545
00:21:03,167 --> 00:21:06,667
Well, look who's finally up.

546
00:21:06,667 --> 00:21:09,667
Wub-wub-wub-
wub-wub-wub-wub!

547
00:21:09,667 --> 00:21:13,100
I really enjoy
our progressive dinners.

548
00:21:13,100 --> 00:21:15,167
Do I just seat myself, then?

549
00:21:17,867 --> 00:21:20,267
Ugh, were you
saving a specimen?

550
00:21:24,267 --> 00:21:26,233
[Chuckles]

551
00:21:26,233 --> 00:21:28,167
No need for that gun.

552
00:21:28,167 --> 00:21:30,067
Sit down.

553
00:21:30,067 --> 00:21:32,233
Have some coffee.
[Coyote howls]

554
00:21:32,233 --> 00:21:33,233
Few beans left.

555
00:21:33,233 --> 00:21:34,767
No need to make a fuss.

556
00:21:34,767 --> 00:21:35,833
Oh, pshaw.

557
00:21:35,833 --> 00:21:39,000
There's always too many beans.

558
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:40,400
I thought
I heard something

559
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:42,267
in the back of that truck
last night.

560
00:21:42,267 --> 00:21:44,833
But I was too lazy
to turn my head and look.

561
00:21:47,067 --> 00:21:49,233
Yeah, thanks, boys,
I really shouldn't,

562
00:21:49,233 --> 00:21:51,933
but, oh, what the heck,
it's Thanksgiving, heh heh.

563
00:21:51,933 --> 00:21:53,200
Fresh ground pepper, sir?

564
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:56,300
My name's cook.
Landis.

565
00:21:56,300 --> 00:21:58,500
We've been up the river.

566
00:21:58,500 --> 00:21:59,633
[Chuckling]

567
00:21:59,633 --> 00:22:00,900
That's my little joke.

568
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:03,967
You sure fooled us, though,
last night.

569
00:22:03,967 --> 00:22:06,467
You must have
been right by his car.

570
00:22:06,467 --> 00:22:08,300
What'd you do?

571
00:22:08,300 --> 00:22:10,767
Grab our tailgate
when we drove off?

572
00:22:10,767 --> 00:22:13,733
Me and landis
got busted back in '58.

573
00:22:13,733 --> 00:22:15,167
Liquor store.

574
00:22:15,167 --> 00:22:17,500
Two years of hard labor.

575
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:20,000
Now we're grabbing a job
anywhere we find it.

576
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:23,500
No more iron cages.

577
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:25,233
Sorry I blew up like that.

578
00:22:25,233 --> 00:22:27,633
Coffee?

579
00:22:27,633 --> 00:22:30,100
And thus
a solid friendship is born.

580
00:22:31,933 --> 00:22:33,800
Ah, that's mighty good
beet juice.

581
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:36,067
Ohh.
[Coyote howling]

582
00:22:36,067 --> 00:22:38,167
This is a hazelnut sumatra
blend.

583
00:22:38,167 --> 00:22:40,500
I think
you'll like it.

584
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:43,167
Anyone for tiramisu?

585
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:49,300
♪♪

586
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:52,467
Oh, no, the lunch rush!

587
00:22:52,467 --> 00:22:54,100
Oh, my ride's here,
gotta get my purse.

588
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,233
Hi, I brought
your new script pages.

589
00:23:04,733 --> 00:23:06,900
Buford pusser,
"walking dense."

590
00:23:06,900 --> 00:23:08,867
You boys just drive down
from the north?

591
00:23:08,867 --> 00:23:10,400
Think, damn it.

592
00:23:10,400 --> 00:23:11,800
Yes, sir.

593
00:23:11,800 --> 00:23:13,000
Mm-hmm.

594
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:14,300
[Imitating Elvis]
Thank you very much.

595
00:23:14,300 --> 00:23:15,800
Didn't happen to see
a big fella,

596
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:18,733
about 200 pounds,
maybe, hitchhiking?

597
00:23:18,733 --> 00:23:21,267
No, sir, we didn't.

598
00:23:21,267 --> 00:23:22,567
Uh-huh.

599
00:23:22,567 --> 00:23:24,933
Do you provide a service
of some sort?

600
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:30,500
Uh-huh.

601
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:32,733
All right, boys,
all right.

602
00:23:32,733 --> 00:23:35,333
Damn, he's good.

603
00:23:35,333 --> 00:23:38,267
Uh, just in case you do,

604
00:23:38,267 --> 00:23:43,000
his name is Griffin,
and the reward is $5,000.

605
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:44,000
[All]
He's over there!

606
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:45,400
Right there!
Right in the bushes!

607
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:50,033
Griffin'll kill you.

608
00:23:50,033 --> 00:23:51,433
Ooh,
kill you, yes.

609
00:23:55,333 --> 00:23:57,367
Gilligan!

610
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:00,367
Oh, oh!

611
00:24:01,467 --> 00:24:05,267
Oh, he's a regular
Mycroft Holmes.

612
00:24:05,267 --> 00:24:09,200
Agh, let me just hoist
my large ass in here, ohh.

613
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:10,900
Once we get past
the character development,

614
00:24:10,900 --> 00:24:12,100
this film's bound
to pick up.

615
00:24:12,100 --> 00:24:14,667
Oh, yeah, heh heh.

616
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:27,933
Ah, yes, yes.

617
00:24:27,933 --> 00:24:30,567
Well, I don't know
about you chaps,

618
00:24:30,567 --> 00:24:33,300
but I'm still in the mood
for some action.

619
00:24:33,300 --> 00:24:36,800
Ah, well, then some high-stakes
pull-tabs, perhaps?

620
00:24:36,800 --> 00:24:39,633
Ooh, I'm getting
a rush already!

621
00:24:39,633 --> 00:24:42,100
Why don't we check
on Dr. Forrester, okay?

622
00:24:42,100 --> 00:24:43,133
Oh, all right.

623
00:24:43,133 --> 00:24:44,633
If you say so.

624
00:24:44,633 --> 00:24:45,800
[Sighs]

625
00:24:45,800 --> 00:24:48,367
So it's come to this, huh?

626
00:24:48,367 --> 00:24:50,067
Are you just
gonna lie there

627
00:24:50,067 --> 00:24:52,567
like a big lump,
feeling sorry for yourself?

628
00:24:52,567 --> 00:24:54,000
Is that it?

629
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,333
Are you gonna let a couple
of scratch and bruises

630
00:24:56,333 --> 00:24:59,000
turn you into a self-pitying
sissypants?

631
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:00,633
Well, are you?!

632
00:25:00,633 --> 00:25:02,033
Are you?!

633
00:25:02,033 --> 00:25:03,800
Look at me
when I'm speaking to you!

634
00:25:03,800 --> 00:25:05,833
[Groaning]

635
00:25:05,833 --> 00:25:07,633
I don't believe this.

636
00:25:07,633 --> 00:25:10,633
I'm very, very disappointed.

637
00:25:10,633 --> 00:25:13,833
The old Clayton forrester
wasn't like this at all.

638
00:25:13,833 --> 00:25:16,333
He didn't know
the meaning of the word quit.

639
00:25:16,333 --> 00:25:18,200
He didn't have it in him!

640
00:25:18,200 --> 00:25:20,067
The old
Dr. Clayton forrester

641
00:25:20,067 --> 00:25:22,767
would have thrown off
these covers.

642
00:25:22,767 --> 00:25:24,067
He would have
pulled himself up!

643
00:25:24,067 --> 00:25:25,300
[Groans loudly]

644
00:25:25,300 --> 00:25:28,433
He would have pulled
his twisted knees over

645
00:25:28,433 --> 00:25:32,667
and stood himself up
on his own two casts.

646
00:25:32,667 --> 00:25:34,100
[Crack]
Agh!

647
00:25:34,100 --> 00:25:37,667
That's the Dr. Clayton forrester
that I know.

648
00:25:37,667 --> 00:25:40,133
But, oh, that posture!

649
00:25:40,133 --> 00:25:41,967
- [Whimpering]
- Here.

650
00:25:41,967 --> 00:25:43,067
[Crack]
Ogh!

651
00:25:43,067 --> 00:25:44,167
- There.
- [Groaning]

652
00:25:44,167 --> 00:25:45,367
Okay, let's have at it.

653
00:25:45,367 --> 00:25:46,933
Let's go, come on.

654
00:25:46,933 --> 00:25:48,867
[Whimpering]

655
00:25:48,867 --> 00:25:49,967
Ugh, man.

656
00:25:49,967 --> 00:25:51,667
I can hardly stand to watch.

657
00:25:51,667 --> 00:25:54,000
Yeah, I know, his posture
is really terrible.

658
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:55,333
The worst!

659
00:25:55,333 --> 00:25:56,900
Yeah, poor frank's
got his work cut out for him.

660
00:25:56,900 --> 00:25:58,500
- That's for sure.
- We'll be right back.

661
00:25:58,500 --> 00:26:00,633
His posture's--
I didn't mean his posture.

662
00:26:08,233 --> 00:26:10,400
[Humming]

663
00:26:10,400 --> 00:26:12,200
He didn't say anything.

664
00:26:12,200 --> 00:26:13,767
Nothin' happened.

665
00:26:13,767 --> 00:26:15,300
You've got it all wrong.

666
00:26:15,300 --> 00:26:17,367
We just made out
for a while.

667
00:26:21,533 --> 00:26:23,267
You were wonderful.

668
00:26:26,167 --> 00:26:29,033
Boy, never before has the screen
been so electrified.

669
00:26:29,033 --> 00:26:30,833
Ah, you were wonderful.

670
00:26:32,533 --> 00:26:34,600
We lead a rich life.

671
00:26:34,600 --> 00:26:37,367
That kind of money's
worth joining up for.

672
00:26:37,367 --> 00:26:40,033
They give you
a thousand bucks to join

673
00:26:40,033 --> 00:26:41,967
and a thousand bucks
when it's over.

674
00:26:41,967 --> 00:26:45,233
That's at least
a thousand bucks.

675
00:26:45,233 --> 00:26:47,400
Maybe it won't be over.

676
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:50,000
Oh, you're deep,
Tony, real deep.

677
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:51,533
Once we get that money
in our hands,

678
00:26:51,533 --> 00:26:53,800
we'll grab
a long freight train.

679
00:26:53,800 --> 00:26:55,633
"Cherokee-Jack"...

680
00:26:55,633 --> 00:26:58,300
Hey, wait, we didn't finish
reading the sign!

681
00:26:58,300 --> 00:27:01,533
U.S. air
needs to update a bit.

682
00:27:01,533 --> 00:27:04,400
Suddenly it's "the best years
of our lives."

683
00:27:04,400 --> 00:27:07,467
[Humming]

684
00:27:07,467 --> 00:27:09,233
Buddy hackett.

685
00:27:09,233 --> 00:27:11,400
Hey.

686
00:27:11,400 --> 00:27:15,267
There'll be a five-hour delay in
our movie, so please stand by.

687
00:27:15,267 --> 00:27:17,800
We'll have the mechanic take
a look at it, and meanwhile,

688
00:27:17,800 --> 00:27:19,700
we'll have to ask you
to board another movie.

689
00:27:22,767 --> 00:27:24,067
He did it again!

690
00:27:24,067 --> 00:27:25,333
You think they'd be
on to him by now.

691
00:27:25,333 --> 00:27:27,633
[Scoffs]

692
00:27:27,633 --> 00:27:30,433
[Humming]

693
00:27:30,433 --> 00:27:32,833
Ah, another
Hollywood pretty boy.

694
00:27:32,833 --> 00:27:37,533
Take a long second and get used
to this face.

695
00:27:37,533 --> 00:27:39,267
Cherokee Jack?

696
00:27:39,267 --> 00:27:41,933
Yeah, I'm cherokee Jack.

697
00:27:41,933 --> 00:27:45,367
Do you know where they train men
to fight down in Cuba?

698
00:27:45,367 --> 00:27:48,200
Yeah, I know
where they train.

699
00:27:48,200 --> 00:27:50,600
How much to fly us
to the training camp?

700
00:27:50,600 --> 00:27:52,367
$15 each.

701
00:27:52,367 --> 00:27:53,667
We're about busted.

702
00:27:53,667 --> 00:27:54,967
What'll you give us
for the truck?

703
00:27:54,967 --> 00:27:56,400
Run good?

704
00:27:56,400 --> 00:27:57,467
Throw in the guy?

705
00:27:57,467 --> 00:27:58,967
Let's look at it.

706
00:27:58,967 --> 00:28:01,000
I'm cherokee Jack.

707
00:28:02,933 --> 00:28:05,467
Oh, no, you ran over my dog!

708
00:28:09,300 --> 00:28:11,000
$35.

709
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:12,000
Mmm, 12,000.

710
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:13,933
$35?

711
00:28:13,933 --> 00:28:15,933
That's the best
I can do, boys.

712
00:28:15,933 --> 00:28:18,867
Well, that's
not too bad.

713
00:28:18,867 --> 00:28:20,533
How about our friend?

714
00:28:20,533 --> 00:28:22,367
Tell you what
I'll do, boys.

715
00:28:22,367 --> 00:28:25,367
You give me the truck,
I'll fly the three of you.

716
00:28:25,367 --> 00:28:27,800
Well, all right,
cherokee.

717
00:28:27,800 --> 00:28:29,433
When can we leave?

718
00:28:29,433 --> 00:28:30,800
Tonight.

719
00:28:30,800 --> 00:28:32,700
I'm cherokee Jack!

720
00:28:32,700 --> 00:28:34,000
It's petey the plane!

721
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:35,267
Hey, petey!

722
00:28:35,267 --> 00:28:36,867
[Propeller rumbling]

723
00:28:36,867 --> 00:28:40,700
Coleman Francis makes
terrific stories.

724
00:28:40,700 --> 00:28:43,633
Well, the close-ups
really save on sets.

725
00:28:43,633 --> 00:28:46,067
That nose wheel
feels mushy.

726
00:28:50,533 --> 00:28:53,600
Uh, weren't they just flying?

727
00:28:53,600 --> 00:28:55,833
Mind if we pick up
my flight instructor?

728
00:28:55,833 --> 00:28:59,367
I've got a few lessons left.

729
00:28:59,367 --> 00:29:00,700
Last known photo.

730
00:29:00,700 --> 00:29:04,200
Just think, the tape deck alone
is worth $35.

731
00:29:04,200 --> 00:29:05,600
Just makes me mad.

732
00:29:05,600 --> 00:29:07,600
We're just gonna roll there,
right?

733
00:29:09,667 --> 00:29:12,400
♪ Da, da da da ♪

734
00:29:12,400 --> 00:29:14,500
♪ da, da da da ah ♪

735
00:29:14,500 --> 00:29:19,167
Look, a soccer team.

736
00:29:19,167 --> 00:29:20,567
If you need any help...

737
00:29:20,567 --> 00:29:22,100
Just press
the stewardess button.

738
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:24,533
... my friend here can fly.

739
00:29:24,533 --> 00:29:27,000
[Thunder crashes]

740
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:29,733
Mind if I smoke?
Thanks, good.

741
00:29:29,733 --> 00:29:31,100
I'll smoke then.

742
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:38,933
You're sitting on my hand.

743
00:29:38,933 --> 00:29:41,600
[Thunder crashes]

744
00:29:43,533 --> 00:29:45,433
It's really
coming down, huh?

745
00:29:45,433 --> 00:29:47,267
Wait a minute,
it's beautiful!

746
00:29:47,267 --> 00:29:49,333
[Laughter]

747
00:29:53,433 --> 00:29:56,933
Mom, they're landing
in our yard!

748
00:29:56,933 --> 00:30:01,067
They've flown all the way
from new Mexico to new Mexico.

749
00:30:01,067 --> 00:30:03,700
I can't handle the truth!

750
00:30:07,433 --> 00:30:09,633
Oh, they're here!

751
00:30:11,267 --> 00:30:13,600
Gah.
Oh, god, that's over.

752
00:30:13,600 --> 00:30:15,000
Ptth! Ohh.

753
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,367
I hope he's here to brief us
on the movie.

754
00:30:24,333 --> 00:30:26,833
I brought you
some fightin' men, Joe.

755
00:30:26,833 --> 00:30:27,967
Good, we can use them.

756
00:30:27,967 --> 00:30:29,833
Okay, men,
follow me.

757
00:30:29,833 --> 00:30:32,100
I'm cherokee Jack.

758
00:30:35,100 --> 00:30:36,933
Well, men, I'll fill you in
on who you are

759
00:30:36,933 --> 00:30:41,333
and where you came from
and why you're here and...

760
00:30:41,333 --> 00:30:44,433
Some of the hottest minders
in Hollywood.

761
00:30:49,467 --> 00:30:51,000
Well, the bears
are acting up,

762
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:53,500
so be careful and keep
your campfires small.

763
00:30:53,500 --> 00:30:56,300
Gentlemen, we shove off
in 24 hours.

764
00:30:56,300 --> 00:30:58,833
- You shove off!
- Haven't time for formalities.

765
00:30:58,833 --> 00:31:02,133
That was an official thing
I just did.

766
00:31:02,133 --> 00:31:05,367
Here's the lunch menu,
just check off what you want.

767
00:31:05,367 --> 00:31:06,733
[Crickets chirping]

768
00:31:06,733 --> 00:31:08,333
Boy, they got
a real cricket problem.

769
00:31:08,333 --> 00:31:09,900
- What's this?
- Sign your name.

770
00:31:09,900 --> 00:31:11,233
What for?

771
00:31:11,233 --> 00:31:13,800
For headquarters
to make out your checks.

772
00:31:13,800 --> 00:31:16,800
Is, uh, "x" okay?

773
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:18,000
We get cash.

774
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,700
- Cherokee said--
- sign your names, please.

775
00:31:22,800 --> 00:31:23,900
[Birds tweeting]

776
00:31:23,900 --> 00:31:26,467
Hey, quit bilocating.

777
00:31:26,467 --> 00:31:27,667
Boy, look at him.

778
00:31:27,667 --> 00:31:30,267
He's guarding the hell
out of that place.

779
00:31:30,267 --> 00:31:34,633
Yes, earth shelter housing is
the choice for guerilla groups.

780
00:31:34,633 --> 00:31:38,900
Remember, guys, no girls
allowed in our fort.

781
00:31:38,900 --> 00:31:40,467
My name's chastain.

782
00:31:40,467 --> 00:31:42,000
Justine?

783
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:43,467
Where you fellas from?

784
00:31:43,467 --> 00:31:44,567
Up north.

785
00:31:44,567 --> 00:31:46,300
Do you know Bob?

786
00:31:46,300 --> 00:31:48,900
Let's get something to eat.

787
00:31:48,900 --> 00:31:50,667
See you around.

788
00:31:50,667 --> 00:31:53,900
I think I really
drew them out.

789
00:31:53,900 --> 00:31:56,667
♪ You can take Salem
out of the country ♪

790
00:31:56,667 --> 00:32:01,667
♪ but, ding, you can't take
the country out of Salem ♪

791
00:32:01,667 --> 00:32:06,033
Ah, my first good shower
in months.

792
00:32:06,033 --> 00:32:08,633
Could you
sponge my back, Steve?

793
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:12,967
Hurry up.

794
00:32:12,967 --> 00:32:15,500
I would never want to go
in after Coleman.

795
00:32:17,800 --> 00:32:21,100
Come on, how long
you gonna take in there?

796
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:22,800
That should be all right.

797
00:32:22,800 --> 00:32:24,200
Yeah?

798
00:32:24,200 --> 00:32:26,700
Yeah, that's perhaps
the logical spot, chastain.

799
00:32:26,700 --> 00:32:30,200
Sergeant justine, I want you
to call me lieutenant Vivian.

800
00:32:30,200 --> 00:32:33,400
We'll hit the beach...
Here.

801
00:32:33,400 --> 00:32:34,933
You smell great, sir.

802
00:32:34,933 --> 00:32:37,600
I'll have Jimmy and the three
that came in tonight.

803
00:32:37,600 --> 00:32:39,067
Those three?

804
00:32:39,067 --> 00:32:40,633
You're right, I'm sorry.

805
00:32:40,633 --> 00:32:42,900
How much help can we expect
from them?

806
00:32:42,900 --> 00:32:44,333
I don't know.

807
00:32:44,333 --> 00:32:47,033
I think we're being watched
by a winslow homer painting.

808
00:32:47,033 --> 00:32:48,567
They're broke.

809
00:32:48,567 --> 00:32:50,233
Probably hiding from the law.

810
00:32:50,233 --> 00:32:52,167
Who knows?

811
00:32:52,167 --> 00:32:54,033
But they are
coming with us.

812
00:32:54,033 --> 00:32:57,867
How much help,
I don't know.

813
00:32:57,867 --> 00:33:00,767
Jose will drop
the ropes... here.

814
00:33:00,767 --> 00:33:02,700
'Cause it's Cuba.

815
00:33:02,700 --> 00:33:05,833
Alive with pleasure.

816
00:33:05,833 --> 00:33:08,833
Ah, they're filming in someone's
basement apartment.

817
00:33:08,833 --> 00:33:12,300
Hmm.

818
00:33:12,300 --> 00:33:15,533
Do you guys think
that justine guy is cute?

819
00:33:20,433 --> 00:33:25,433
Must be a railroad highway
somewhere around here.

820
00:33:25,433 --> 00:33:30,033
I'd do five years to get that
cherokee throat in my hands.

821
00:33:30,033 --> 00:33:32,000
Nothing I hate
more than a liar.

822
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:34,133
Don't worry...
We'll get out of here.

823
00:33:34,133 --> 00:33:37,300
Well, I feel better
just talking about it.

824
00:33:37,300 --> 00:33:39,933
Thanks, guys.

825
00:33:39,933 --> 00:33:42,267
Our auteur,
ladies and gentlemen.

826
00:33:45,433 --> 00:33:47,400
Honey, you up?

827
00:33:55,067 --> 00:33:56,133
[Knock on door]

828
00:33:56,133 --> 00:33:58,100
Turn-down service.

829
00:33:58,100 --> 00:33:59,700
Come in, chastain.

830
00:34:03,933 --> 00:34:07,300
Hour after hour
of heart-pounding small talk.

831
00:34:07,300 --> 00:34:11,133
♪♪

832
00:34:11,133 --> 00:34:16,600
So you guys want to mess around
and overthrow Cuba or something?

833
00:34:16,600 --> 00:34:18,400
Nice evening.

834
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:20,300
What can we
do for you?

835
00:34:20,300 --> 00:34:22,800
Oh, it's getting
sort of lonely.

836
00:34:22,800 --> 00:34:24,867
So I thought I'd get
some news from civilization.

837
00:34:24,867 --> 00:34:27,000
Arizona, maybe.

838
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:28,833
Arizona, huh?

839
00:34:28,833 --> 00:34:30,433
Out around Pearl.

840
00:34:30,433 --> 00:34:31,733
They have a ranch there.

841
00:34:31,733 --> 00:34:33,500
How'd you get caught
in that?

842
00:34:33,500 --> 00:34:35,333
My grandparents
live in Cuba.

843
00:34:35,333 --> 00:34:36,900
Their sugar mill
was taken away.

844
00:34:36,900 --> 00:34:39,067
Now it's a nutrasweet mill.

845
00:34:39,067 --> 00:34:40,667
Oh, I don't know, i--

846
00:34:40,667 --> 00:34:43,133
I figured they
have a right to it.

847
00:34:43,133 --> 00:34:45,233
You got your first money?

848
00:34:45,233 --> 00:34:46,867
Cash?

849
00:34:46,867 --> 00:34:48,700
Afraid not--
this one is on me.

850
00:34:48,700 --> 00:34:50,633
What does that mean?

851
00:34:50,633 --> 00:34:51,833
"What does that mean?"

852
00:34:51,833 --> 00:34:53,000
It means I'm gonna try
and help my grandparents

853
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:55,133
get their mill back.

854
00:34:55,133 --> 00:34:57,433
It sounds sort of foolish,
doesn't it?

855
00:34:57,433 --> 00:34:59,433
- Nice plywood guitar.
- [Giggles]

856
00:34:59,433 --> 00:35:00,567
Guess I'm a dreamer.

857
00:35:00,567 --> 00:35:02,300
Aren't we all?

858
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:05,267
My wife, Ruby,
says I'm a dreamer.

859
00:35:05,267 --> 00:35:08,767
They financed me four years
of college.

860
00:35:08,767 --> 00:35:10,100
Figured, oh,
it was something--

861
00:35:10,100 --> 00:35:11,600
but enough of that.

862
00:35:11,600 --> 00:35:13,233
[Chuckles]

863
00:35:14,400 --> 00:35:15,667
Ah, I know.

864
00:35:15,667 --> 00:35:20,333
This is the gomer pyle exhibit
at filmways land.

865
00:35:20,333 --> 00:35:23,767
"Full metal curly."

866
00:35:23,767 --> 00:35:25,567
I always wanted money...

867
00:35:25,567 --> 00:35:26,733
But I settled
for looks.

868
00:35:26,733 --> 00:35:28,033
... a lot of money.

869
00:35:28,033 --> 00:35:32,000
If we stick together,
maybe we can get money.

870
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:33,367
Somewhere.

871
00:35:33,367 --> 00:35:35,667
Where does the pillow end
and his face begin?

872
00:35:35,667 --> 00:35:38,433
If the boys back at the joint
could see me now.

873
00:35:38,433 --> 00:35:41,000
Aw, Coleman hogged
all the best lines for himself.

874
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:43,700
Going to fight for some peasants
in Cuba.

875
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:46,467
[Laughing]

876
00:35:46,467 --> 00:35:49,267
I had to finish smoking before
I could find that amusing.

877
00:35:49,267 --> 00:35:52,200
Griffin, how did you
bust out?

878
00:35:52,200 --> 00:35:53,900
You never told us
much about that.

879
00:35:53,900 --> 00:35:56,467
[Clearing throat]

880
00:35:59,033 --> 00:36:00,867
Drain pipe.

881
00:36:00,867 --> 00:36:01,967
Dug up some dirt.

882
00:36:01,967 --> 00:36:03,833
Shut up, everyone,
he's gonna tell the dirt

883
00:36:03,833 --> 00:36:05,467
and the drain pipe story,
shh!

884
00:36:05,467 --> 00:36:07,967
I worked
three long months.

885
00:36:07,967 --> 00:36:09,667
Griffin.

886
00:36:09,667 --> 00:36:12,933
Seems like
I read about a Griffin once.

887
00:36:12,933 --> 00:36:15,600
They called him the cotton king
of the south.

888
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:17,567
This film
dares you to watch it.

889
00:36:17,567 --> 00:36:20,433
Sold a lot of cotton one day.

890
00:36:20,433 --> 00:36:25,633
That night, sent his trucks in
and stole it right back.

891
00:36:25,633 --> 00:36:29,500
They sent him up
for a long stretch.

892
00:36:29,500 --> 00:36:33,500
It seems like
a thousand years ago.

893
00:36:33,500 --> 00:36:38,100
His wife spent all the money
and become a streetwalker.

894
00:36:38,100 --> 00:36:41,800
Every newspaper in the south
had a picture of her.

895
00:36:41,800 --> 00:36:43,400
Well, that's a weird stain.

896
00:36:43,400 --> 00:36:48,533
A beautiful broad.

897
00:36:48,533 --> 00:36:49,733
♪♪

898
00:36:49,733 --> 00:36:52,233
Ah! It's my incessant droning,
isn't it?

899
00:36:52,233 --> 00:36:53,600
That's it, yeah.

900
00:36:56,300 --> 00:36:59,233
There, now can we
get some sleep?

901
00:37:00,433 --> 00:37:03,167
I'm just in a bad mood,
I'm sorry.

902
00:37:03,167 --> 00:37:06,900
[Sniffling]
I'm so sorry.

903
00:37:06,900 --> 00:37:09,133
May I finish my story?

904
00:37:14,133 --> 00:37:17,867
I think you're supposed
to strangle me till I'm dead.

905
00:37:17,867 --> 00:37:21,433
This is gonna be hard to ignore,
fella.

906
00:37:21,433 --> 00:37:23,133
[Groaning]

907
00:37:23,133 --> 00:37:24,400
- Ooh.
- Oh?

908
00:37:24,400 --> 00:37:25,800
We've got the gravel pit
till noon,

909
00:37:25,800 --> 00:37:27,700
and then the sandinistas
are scheduled.

910
00:37:27,700 --> 00:37:29,400
[Humming]

911
00:37:29,400 --> 00:37:30,533
Hey, Van cliburn.

912
00:37:30,533 --> 00:37:31,867
[Laughing]

913
00:37:31,867 --> 00:37:35,067
Ha, they're at bus driver
training!

914
00:37:35,067 --> 00:37:36,200
Hi-Cuba!

915
00:37:36,200 --> 00:37:38,033
[Guffaws]

916
00:37:38,033 --> 00:37:40,700
Ah, Castro doesn't
stand a chance.

917
00:37:42,900 --> 00:37:44,733
Okay, now,
what we want to do here is--

918
00:37:44,733 --> 00:37:45,867
hey, where are you going?

919
00:37:45,867 --> 00:37:50,467
Do you really need
to practice jumping?

920
00:37:50,467 --> 00:37:53,233
Uhh! All right, I'm sitting
on a spike-- you can't move me.

921
00:37:53,233 --> 00:37:54,700
[Grunting]

922
00:37:54,700 --> 00:37:56,167
Ooh.

923
00:37:56,167 --> 00:37:59,667
It's the shining path
fantasy camp.

924
00:37:59,667 --> 00:38:01,533
[Humming]

925
00:38:01,533 --> 00:38:04,533
Les steckel
begins training camp.

926
00:38:04,533 --> 00:38:06,400
♪♪

927
00:38:06,400 --> 00:38:09,600
[Humming]

928
00:38:09,600 --> 00:38:11,133
Uhh! Oof!

929
00:38:11,133 --> 00:38:12,167
[Giggles]

930
00:38:12,167 --> 00:38:16,233
I don't want
to be covert anymore.

931
00:38:16,233 --> 00:38:20,733
We do more before noon than most
people do before 10:00!

932
00:38:22,300 --> 00:38:23,900
That was very good, Jimmy.

933
00:38:23,900 --> 00:38:27,067
"That was very good,
Jimmy."

934
00:38:27,067 --> 00:38:31,967
It was nice of Mrs. Mimbock
to let them use her back yard.

935
00:38:31,967 --> 00:38:33,967
We're gonna make it.

936
00:38:33,967 --> 00:38:35,933
Now's the time.

937
00:38:35,933 --> 00:38:37,933
We'll hide out
till they shove off.

938
00:38:37,933 --> 00:38:40,567
You shove off!

939
00:38:40,567 --> 00:38:42,133
Coleman's think tank.

940
00:38:42,133 --> 00:38:43,633
[Chuckles]

941
00:38:43,633 --> 00:38:46,400
Let's make a run for it.

942
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:48,100
Well, make a jog for it,
anyway.

943
00:38:48,100 --> 00:38:49,400
Halt!

944
00:38:49,400 --> 00:38:52,800
And their highly-detailed plan
falls apart.

945
00:38:52,800 --> 00:38:56,933
Ah, the revolution
is highly underfunded.

946
00:38:56,933 --> 00:38:59,233
Not me.

947
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:04,333
Let Jimmy try his look.

948
00:39:04,333 --> 00:39:05,967
Come on, ed,
roll 'em.

949
00:39:05,967 --> 00:39:08,000
Hey, where Tom Arnold
lives now.

950
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:09,967
[Laughter]

951
00:39:09,967 --> 00:39:11,533
Oh, they got caught

952
00:39:11,533 --> 00:39:13,467
and now they're forced to just
hang out somewhere, huh?

953
00:39:13,467 --> 00:39:14,567
Come on, boy.

954
00:39:14,567 --> 00:39:15,700
Come on,
treat your daddy right.

955
00:39:15,700 --> 00:39:16,733
I need a pair of shoes.

956
00:39:16,733 --> 00:39:19,033
And a hat
and a purse.

957
00:39:19,033 --> 00:39:21,100
- Yahtzee!
- Snake eyes.

958
00:39:24,333 --> 00:39:25,700
[Humming]

959
00:39:25,700 --> 00:39:27,667
Hey, it's fee waybill.

960
00:39:27,667 --> 00:39:30,600
You boys want to play
some gallopin' dominos?

961
00:39:30,600 --> 00:39:31,933
What?

962
00:39:31,933 --> 00:39:33,667
You wanna shoot some dice?

963
00:39:33,667 --> 00:39:36,200
Maybe play
some freeze tag?

964
00:39:37,700 --> 00:39:38,867
Agh, I see!

965
00:39:38,867 --> 00:39:41,133
Well, perhaps I'll ask you
again later, ow.

966
00:39:41,133 --> 00:39:43,667
Men, I want you to study
this picture my son drew.

967
00:39:43,667 --> 00:39:45,400
Men, we're shoving off
right after sundown.

968
00:39:45,400 --> 00:39:46,700
[All]
You shove off!

969
00:39:46,700 --> 00:39:48,367
I wanted to give you some idea
of what to expect.

970
00:39:48,367 --> 00:39:50,800
There's 80,000 of them
and seven of us.

971
00:39:50,800 --> 00:39:52,833
At 12:00 midnight,
we hit the beach.

972
00:39:52,833 --> 00:39:56,167
At 12:30, a patrol boat
makes its nightly run.

973
00:39:56,167 --> 00:39:59,333
We have 30 minutes
to scale 80-foot cliffs

974
00:39:59,333 --> 00:40:00,433
and clear the beach.

975
00:40:00,433 --> 00:40:01,867
Ted, you take Havana.

976
00:40:01,867 --> 00:40:03,667
We fight if we have to.

977
00:40:03,667 --> 00:40:06,800
Our job is to tear down
telephone lines,

978
00:40:06,800 --> 00:40:10,967
destroy all forms of
communication, ammo dumps.

979
00:40:10,967 --> 00:40:12,767
We have a man in Cuba

980
00:40:12,767 --> 00:40:16,200
that'll pull ropes over
the cliff at 12:00 midnight.

981
00:40:16,200 --> 00:40:18,200
At 12:15, we're captured.

982
00:40:18,200 --> 00:40:20,167
We must not
get caught on the beach.

983
00:40:20,167 --> 00:40:22,233
Especially with my thighs.
[Shudders]

984
00:40:22,233 --> 00:40:24,000
The invasion force
will follow us.

985
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:25,633
They should
be there at daybreak.

986
00:40:25,633 --> 00:40:27,067
Any questions?

987
00:40:27,067 --> 00:40:29,867
Yeah, are your bunkmates
supposed to strangle you?

988
00:40:31,667 --> 00:40:33,600
Okay, men, that's it.

989
00:40:33,600 --> 00:40:35,500
Start getting
your gear ready.

990
00:40:35,500 --> 00:40:36,700
Gear?

991
00:40:36,700 --> 00:40:38,533
Be ready to shove off
right after sundown.

992
00:40:38,533 --> 00:40:40,100
[All]
You shove off!

993
00:40:42,033 --> 00:40:43,967
Careful with those cigars.

994
00:40:46,833 --> 00:40:48,933
It's the ghost brigade.

995
00:40:48,933 --> 00:40:52,300
[Humming marching music]

996
00:40:52,300 --> 00:40:54,133
Wow, what
an awesome sight.

997
00:40:54,133 --> 00:40:55,700
Uh-huh.

998
00:41:05,600 --> 00:41:07,067
♪ A kiss on the hand ♪

999
00:41:07,067 --> 00:41:09,700
♪ may be quite continental ♪

1000
00:41:09,700 --> 00:41:11,367
Uh, Mike?

1001
00:41:11,367 --> 00:41:13,433
What are you doing?

1002
00:41:13,433 --> 00:41:14,933
Oh, I'm sorry,
Mike's not here.

1003
00:41:14,933 --> 00:41:16,733
I'm Carol Channing.

1004
00:41:16,733 --> 00:41:20,333
I'd be glad to bring a message
to this-- Mike, was it?

1005
00:41:20,333 --> 00:41:22,000
Oh, I see.

1006
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:25,533
Oh, this movie must have really
gotten to you, poor sap.

1007
00:41:25,533 --> 00:41:27,767
[Imitating Carol Channing]
Well, it's just sensational

1008
00:41:27,767 --> 00:41:29,567
that you're
Carol Channing!

1009
00:41:29,567 --> 00:41:30,733
- [Laughing]
- What are you doing?

1010
00:41:30,733 --> 00:41:32,333
Hey, stop that.

1011
00:41:32,333 --> 00:41:35,233
As Carol Channing, I must say,
that is very annoying.

1012
00:41:35,233 --> 00:41:36,267
Ohh.

1013
00:41:36,267 --> 00:41:38,900
We better check
on frank and Dr. F., eh?

1014
00:41:38,900 --> 00:41:39,900
Oh, yeah, yeah.

1015
00:41:39,900 --> 00:41:41,033
Well, you do whatever
you want.

1016
00:41:41,033 --> 00:41:43,667
I'm lunching
with Angela lansbury.

1017
00:41:43,667 --> 00:41:44,767
- Uh...
- Uh...

1018
00:41:44,767 --> 00:41:47,700
Heh heh heh.
I, uh...

1019
00:41:47,700 --> 00:41:50,133
Happy talk,
talk about the things--

1020
00:41:50,133 --> 00:41:51,333
oh, hi!

1021
00:41:51,333 --> 00:41:53,867
Well, I guess
it's pretty inevitable now.

1022
00:41:53,867 --> 00:41:56,333
Basically, it's a waiting game
at this point.

1023
00:41:56,333 --> 00:41:59,400
I've decided not to employ
any radical procedures.

1024
00:41:59,400 --> 00:42:00,400
Water.

1025
00:42:00,400 --> 00:42:01,800
Sorry, Clay.

1026
00:42:01,800 --> 00:42:03,100
It's pretty hopeless.

1027
00:42:03,100 --> 00:42:05,033
Been going through your mail,
though.

1028
00:42:05,033 --> 00:42:06,300
Oh, look at this.

1029
00:42:06,300 --> 00:42:09,300
It's a card
from Leo buscaglia.

1030
00:42:09,300 --> 00:42:12,367
"Hope you die,
you rotten bast..."

1031
00:42:12,367 --> 00:42:14,033
[Chuckling]
Oh, well.

1032
00:42:14,033 --> 00:42:16,600
[Phone ringing]

1033
00:42:16,600 --> 00:42:18,900
- Hello?
- Hello, frank?

1034
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:20,167
It's Jimmy Carter.

1035
00:42:20,167 --> 00:42:21,333
Yes, Mr. President?

1036
00:42:21,333 --> 00:42:22,467
Is he dead yet?

